---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'The Big Three', href: '/explore/the-big-three/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Big Three — Sun, Moon, and Rising Sign"
  description="Your Sun, Moon, and rising sign explained in plain language: what each one actually governs, why they differ, and why the rising sign needs your exact birth time."
  path="/explore/the-big-three/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The Big Three: Sun, Moon, and rising</h1>
        <p class="lede">Three placements do most of the introductions. Here's what each one is actually responsible for — and why knowing all three explains why sun-sign horoscopes only half fit.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.ripplesSunset.src}
        srcset={ocean.ripplesSunset.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.ripplesSunset.alt}
        width={ocean.ripplesSunset.width}
        height={ocean.ripplesSunset.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        Your <strong>Sun</strong> is what you're here to become. Your <strong>Moon</strong> is what
        you need to feel safe. Your <strong>rising sign</strong> is how you meet the world and how
        the world first meets you. Everyone has all three, and they are frequently in different
        signs — which is exactly why a single sun-sign horoscope can feel about forty percent
        accurate.
      </p>

      <h2>The Sun: your becoming</h2>
      <p>
        When someone asks "what's your sign," they mean your Sun — the zodiac sign the Sun occupied
        on your birthday. It changes roughly monthly, so it's the one placement you can know without
        a birth time or place.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Sun describes purpose and vitality: the qualities you grow into rather than the ones you
        arrive with. This is why sun-sign descriptions sometimes feel aspirational rather than
        literal — they describe a direction of travel. A Capricorn Sun isn't necessarily disciplined
        at twenty-two; it's a person whose life will keep handing them lessons in building
        something that lasts. Read more about
        <a href="/explore/planets/sun/">the Sun in astrology</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={ocean.lightRays} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The Moon: your needs</h2>
      <p>
        The Moon moves fast — through the whole zodiac in about 27 days, roughly two and a half days
        per sign — so your Moon sign depends on your birth date and, for people born near a sign
        change, the time of day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where the Sun is public, the Moon is private. It governs instinct, emotional weather,
        comfort, memory, and what "home" has to include before you can rest. People often recognize
        their Moon sign faster than their Sun, because it describes the self that shows up when
        nobody's performing. If your Sun and Moon are in signs that want opposite things — a
        Sagittarius Sun with a Cancer Moon, say — that internal negotiation is a genuine feature of
        your life, not a contradiction to resolve. See
        <a href="/explore/planets/moon/">the Moon in astrology</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak
    image={ocean.seaStackMoonrise}
    line="Where the Sun is public, the Moon is private."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The rising sign: your threshold</h2>
      <p>
        The rising sign — or Ascendant — is the zodiac sign that was climbing over the eastern
        horizon at the moment and place you were born. Because the Earth rotates, it changes roughly
        every two hours, which makes it the most time-sensitive of the three.
      </p>
      <p>
        It does two jobs. First, it describes your approach: the manner you lead with, the
        impression you make before anyone knows you, the instinctive way you handle a new room.
        Second — and this is the part beginners rarely hear — it sets the entire
        <a href="/explore/houses/">house structure</a> of your chart. Your rising sign determines
        which area of life each planet operates in. Two people born the same day in the same city
        four hours apart share nearly every planet-in-sign placement and live them in completely
        different arenas.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          <strong>This is why astrologers ask for your exact birth time.</strong> Without it, the Sun
          and usually the Moon are still reliable, but the rising sign and the houses are guesswork.
          <a href="/prepare-for-your-reading/">Here's how to track your birth time down.</a>
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Reading them together</h2>
      <p>
        The useful move is not to collect three labels but to notice how they interact. A Leo Sun
        with a Virgo Moon and Scorpio rising is someone with a generous, creative purpose, a private
        need for order and usefulness, and a guarded, penetrating first impression. That's already a
        recognizable person — and it's still only three placements out of a chart that holds ten
        planets, twelve houses, and the aspects between them.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the Big Three interest you, the rest of the chart is where it gets specific. Start with
        <a href="/explore/birth-chart-basics/">birth chart basics</a>, or open the
        <a href="/tools/explore-your-chart/">interactive chart explorer</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Curious what your whole chart says?"
    body="A natal reading goes well past the big three — every planet, house, and aspect, connected to your actual life."
    buttonLabel="About the Natal Reading"
    buttonHref="/readings/natal/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
